  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nShort Term: Monday...\n\nSlightly cooler temperatures will come Monday (upper 30s to low 40s \nhighs) as surface winds remain light (5-10 mph). These light winds \nwill become easterly as an around 1030mb surface high pressure \ncenter slides by to the northeast (feature contributing to the \nongoing light/calm winds). Aloft, the flow remains fairly zonal \n(flat west to east flow), helping keep the local conditions dry for \nnow. The next weak precipitation chance, which used to reside Monday \nnight into Tuesday, has virtually vanished given recent pessimistic \nmodel guidance. \n\n\nLong Term: Tuesday through Saturday...\n\nThe period of cold temperatures (highs mainly in the 40s) should \nextend across the middle off the week (through Thursday) as weak \nsoutherly winds battle against partly cloudy to overcast skies. \nThough a weak shortwave appears possible to sweep by the \nnorth/central plains on Wednesday, the chance for precipitation \noutside of a few flurries remain non-existant at this time. In fact, \nthe forecast through the rest of the week technically remains dry. \nThe moisture concentration overall (PWATS <0.5\") does not appear to \nbe all that supportive for precipitation in general.\n\nThe upper-level pattern behind the disturbance Wednesday currently \nlooks to become dominated by northwest flow (patten suggested by \nboth the GFS and ECMWF). With the southerly retaining surface winds \npotentially gaining a westerly component again Thursday, downslope \nflow from off the High Plains could help bump highs back to the 50s \nby the end of the week. Looking past the 7-day forecast, a faint \nsignal with the long-range ensemble models (LREF) seems to keep any \nwarmup up at the end of the week short-lived as yet another cold \nsnap appears to be eminent around the start of next week.",
